# Line endings. Every text file is checked out with LF on every platform,
# Windows included. This is not a style preference - two things in this
# repository are compiled from file *bytes*, so a CRLF checkout changes what
# ships:
#
#   - `libs/skills/**/*.md` are embedded with `include_str!` (`ai/skills.rs`).
#     `split_frontmatter` looks for the literal `---\n`, so with CRLF the whole
#     frontmatter block is silently dropped: `job-identify` loses its model
#     tier, `cv-import` loses its description. Every Windows build shipped so
#     far carries that bug.
#   - `apps/desktop/src-tauri/migrations/*.sql` are hashed by `sqlx::migrate!`
#     at compile time, so CRLF produces different checksums than macOS or
#     Linux, and `db.rs`'s pinned-checksum test fails there.
#
# Both were invisible until the `Windows check` workflow first compiled the
# crate on Windows. Consequence of fixing it, recorded on purpose: a Windows
# install made before this change recorded CRLF checksums in `_sqlx_migrations`
# and will refuse to migrate against the LF ones. That is a reinstall, and it is
# cheap now in a way it will not be later.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Belt and braces for the formats git's content detection could get wrong, and
# for the media that is deliberately not in LFS.
*.png -text
*.jpg -text
*.jpeg -text
*.gif -text
*.ico -text
*.icns -text
*.webp -text
*.mp4 -text
*.zip -text
*.ttf -text
*.otf -text
*.woff -text
*.woff2 -text
*.pdf -text
*.docx -text
*.xlsx -text

# Git LFS. The guide's screenshots and screen recordings are binary and get
# retaken, so every version git stores is a whole extra copy that can only be
# removed by rewriting history. Pointers keep the history light; the bytes live
# in LFS and are fetched only for the revision being checked out.
#
# History was rewritten once, over v0.25.0..main, to move the media already
# committed there into LFS as well. No tag moved and no release changed: every
# guide asset was added after v0.25.0, which is the last tag. That rewrite is
# not to be repeated - it was cheap only because the repository was private,
# unforked and had a single working copy.
#
# LFS hooks live in .husky/, not .git/hooks, because this repository sets
# core.hooksPath. See .husky/pre-push.
apps/web/public/guide/*.mp4 filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
apps/web/public/guide/*.gif filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
apps/web/public/guide/*.png filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
# The README media under docs/assets is deliberately NOT in LFS, and that is a
# reversal of the original reasoning here.
#
# The old rule was right about storage and wrong about bandwidth. GitHub does
# resolve LFS pointers when it renders markdown - the READMEs work either way -
# but every render that misses the image cache is an LFS download, and the six
# screenshots, the hero banner, the walkthrough thumbnail and the demo GIF come
# to roughly 4 MB of a single README view. The free LFS allowance is 1 GB of
# bandwidth a month, and exceeding it does not degrade gracefully: GitHub
# disables LFS downloads for the whole repository, which breaks every image in
# every README *and* makes `git clone` fail, at exactly the moment a launch is
# sending people to look.
#
# Plain Git blobs are served from the same CDN with no metered allowance, so
# these files cost ~4.5 MB in the pack once and nothing thereafter. Retakes do
# accumulate in history, which is the price: keep them infrequent, and prefer
# replacing a capture over adding a near-duplicate beside it.
#
# The guide's media above stays in LFS: it is served to visitors by Cloudflare
# Pages out of the build, not by GitHub, so it never touches the allowance.
# The press kit is a rebuild of assets tracked above, so it changes whenever any
# of them does and every version Git stored in full would be a second copy of
# media that is already in LFS once.
apps/web/public/press/*.zip filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
